{"qid":"Q188161","names":["Dutch East Indies"],"title":"Dutch East Indies","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Dutch East Indies was the colonial territory of the Netherlands across the Indonesian archipelago, formally constituted in 1800 when the bankrupt Dutch East India Company (VOC) was dissolved and its possessions nationalized. At its administrative apex under the Ethical Policy era (1901–1942), it encompassed Sumatra, Java, Kalimantan, Sulawesi, and much of what is now eastern Indonesia, making it one of the most economically productive colonies in the world through exports of sugar, rubber, tobacco, and petroleum. Japanese forces occupied the territory from March 1942 to August 1945; after Japan's surrender, Sukarno proclaimed Indonesian independence on 17 August 1945, and following four years of armed struggle and diplomatic pressure, the Netherlands transferred sovereignty to the Republic of Indonesia on 27 December 1949.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Colonial administrative boundaries are well documented through Dutch government records and surveys; extent of effective control varied considerably outside Java, where indirect rule through local rulers introduced ambiguity in frontier regions.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Dutch East Indies","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Dutch-East-Indies"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Dutch East Indies","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_East_Indies"}],"britannica":"place/Dutch-East-Indies","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Dutch East Indies","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Johannes Siberg","nativeName":"Johannes Siberg","roman":"Johannes Sibe